          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Partition of Bengal 1905</strong></p>",
            "<p>Lord Curzon partitioned Bengal into East &amp; West; massive protests; Swadeshi Movement began</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Swadeshi Movement</strong></p>",
            "<p>1905 — boycott of British goods; use of Indian products; started from Bengal</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Partition Revoked</strong></p>",
            "<p>1911 — Bengal reunited; capital shifted from Calcutta to Delhi</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Alipore Bomb Case 1908</strong></p>",
            "<p>Aurobindo Ghosh &amp; Bal Gangadhar Tilak involved; Bengal revolutionaries</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Chittagong Armoury Raid</strong></p>",
            "<p>1930 — Surya Sen (\"Master Da\") led raid; major revolutionary act in Bengal</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Jalianwala Bagh Link</strong></p>",
            "<p>Rabindranath Tagore returned knighthood in protest</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Quit India 1942</strong></p>",
            "<p>Bengal was major centre; widespread protests; Midnapore (Tamluk) formed parallel govt</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Tamluk National Govt</strong></p>",
            "<p>1942 — Satish Samanta led parallel government in Tamluk (East Midnapore) for 2 years</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Bengal Famine 1943</strong></p>",
            "<p>Worst famine; 30 lakh+ died; caused by Churchill's policies; deeply affected Bengal's psyche</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
